[{:seon.task/name "a-check-run-never-loses-the-seed"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `bin/check e2e loop` given a relative output path left the store's `:db` branch at 1,282 datoms instead of the seeded ~5,234, and every later `bin/check smoke` on it failed (`n-vars` 42, expected over 360); a fresh store passed. Correct: find what writes to `:db` (a check may only branch from it); `seon.e2e/loop-run` takes its directory as a required argument now ([dir])."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-run-leaves-the-seed-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-watch-waiter"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: two watch-waiters. `seon.check/await-ref` (test/seon/check.clj, private, any reference: add a watch, check once, deref a promise), called on two atoms of `seon.check` and on nothing else, and `seon.root/await-db` (src, main's connection), which does the same and, for a named wait, also ends on the reconciler's quiet (:seon.root/quiet-before-true). e2e calls `seon.root/await-db` directly (its alias c); seon.await holds only `seon.await/await-quiet`. So src cannot define await-db over await-ref, which lives in test/, and only await-ref's watch-and-deref is common. Correct: the common part in one place src can reach, with await-ref calling it, or await-ref left as it is if that part is not worth a var (about 6 lines). The proof named below is about a lease's verdict and does not tell the two apart; this is a tidy with no failing proof."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/lease-ends-with-a-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-stops-on-a-signal-not-on-stdin"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: seon.check/root-serve loops (read-line) until its stdin closes, and bin/root stop ends a root by closing that pipe (bin/held's TERM closes the root's stdin): stdin as a lifecycle, AGENTS section 9's mistake, with one cause for two ends (a stop and a vanished holder). Correct: the root ends on a signal (its shutdown hooks already stop it on SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP: seon.check/root-serve's end!), bin/held sends TERM to the root's process (group) at its stop, the holder keeps no pipe to the root's stdin, and root-serve parks on the stop's promise instead of reading stdin (bin/mcp's relay and the owner's `bin/root stop` unchanged). Proof: bin/root stop on a root idle for 5 s ends it cleanly (ROOT STOPPED, exit 0) with the root's stdin /dev/null; a SIGKILLed holder ends it through the starter's fifo. Needs bin/held on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-root-stop-root-idle-5-s-stdin}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hand-started-root-has-a-starter-or-says-it-stands"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner decides between requiring `bin/root start` to say it is standing and inheriting the session's starter, after the evidence below is gathered. Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: a root started without SEON_STARTER_FIFO does not die with its starter (the owner's standing root is meant to outlive the shell that started it; a root a worktree session starts by hand from its tool shell is not). bin/check and every test root always name a starter fifo (bin/held; seon.test-store/root-sh; the one-launcher check proves no test launches a root another way), so what remains is the hand start. Correct, a decision before code: either `bin/root start` requires its caller to say which it is (a standing root: --standing; any other names the starter, or is refused with the fifo it needs), or a root started from a session's tool shell inherits that session's starter. Evidence to gather first: how many `bin/root start` calls in session transcripts were never followed by a stop, and what each held (the 8 `seon lease` roots of 2026-10-23 were bin/check's, now covered). Proof: the chosen form refuses or ends an unnamed, unstanding root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-runner-sees-its-childs-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (agent-view): a bin/test shard runner (test/test_runner.clj --shard, pid 44597) waited 1 h 55 m on a process test whose root process was gone: its worktree's target/seon was rebuilt under the running test. Nothing recorded that the child had ended; the shard never reported, and bin/test never ended. A wait for something that never came is a recorded fact, never a hang (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs). Correct: a process test's waits (the binary root it starts, its agents) end on the child's exit or socket EOF, and the test fails with that reason (which process, its exit status, the last it said), so the shard reports and bin/test ends. Proof: a process test whose root is killed mid-test fails at once naming the exit, and the shard's result says so."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-process-test-whose-root-dies-fails-with-the-exit}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-worktree-syncs-every-submodule-its-branch-pins"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): `bin/worktree --sync NAME` moves only the submodules the worktree already has a checkout of; one main added after the worktree was made (reference-code/llama.cpp) is skipped without a word (its loop's `[[ -e $WT/$sub/.git ]] || continue`), so after merging main the worktree builds and tests without it and nothing says so. Correct: --sync does what setup does for a submodule it lacks (a worktree of the main tree's submodule repo at the branch's pin, and its nested copies), and says so; one it cannot make (the main tree has not checked it out, or lacks the pin) is named and the command exits non-zero, never silent. Proof: a worktree made before a submodule was added gets it from --sync, and one the main tree lacks makes --sync fail with its name."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-worktree-sync-worktree-made-before-submodule-was}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-single-mode-drops-the-main-it-worked-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 (full-run-reds): `bin/check durable-write check-durable` run twice by itself hangs the second time. Cause: bin/check's single mode drops `check-<mode>` (check-durable-write) before and after, but durable-write, durable-read, window-write and window-read work on the main their argument names (check-durable), so that main and the branches made under it (:dur-a, :dur-b, :dur-c, :dur-clone) stay in target/check-store. The next run's clone! is refused (\"Branch already exists.\") and a1's merge is behind (11 keys). A full run is unaffected (its own stores under its run directory). Correct: a single mode drops the main its run works on (the argument when the mode takes one), so a mode run twice by itself is the same run; seon.check/ready! now refuses on a refused start, so this ends red rather than hanging. Proof: two successive `bin/check durable-write check-durable` and `bin/check durable-read check-durable` pairs, both OK."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/two-successive-bin-check-durable-write-check-durable}}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-waits-for-every-input-in-flight"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 (full-run-reds): the full run's idle was red under load (`:steps-at-idle {:render [5 6]}`, 16 bytes: one chunked `: settled` frame) and green alone. seon.check/idle now waits for the render proc's asks in flight (seon.render.proc/stats :pending, read by Flow's ping: the step that answers the last sends a settled mark). Not covered: a commit notice still on its way to the render proc (`:render-main` in the report, the render proc's :main-t behind main's t), whose step may send nothing the tab sees, so there is no mark to wait for. Correct: a barrier that answers after the render proc has taken main's commits up to a t, in order (as fork-server-caught-up does for the fork server), so the window opens on a render proc that holds main. If the red comes back, the report's :render-pending and :render-main name which case it was."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-check-idle-under-load-answers-only-after}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-paused-proc-takes-nothing-in-backpressure"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 18:20 in bin/prove --merge of jolt/grep-says-what-it-ignored (a change no closer to Flow than a contract's message; batch 82's merge proof had it green), load ~8 with a game on 3 cores: `bin/check backpressure` red on :ring-pushes-back alone, with :taken-before-the-pause 0 and :taken-while-paused 1 (seon.check's backpressure step: the ping after the proc's pause read 0 noise, the ping after :seon.config.check/hold-ms read 1). The flood had begun (the :started event) but the proc had taken none of it when the pause arrived, then took one while paused. Either a step in flight when the pause arrived (Flow's control channel is FIFO between steps, so a step already reading its ins completes one take), or the first ping answering before the pause took effect. Learn: core.async Flow's proc loop (reference-code, flow/impl) for when a :pause is read against a pending take; reproduce at n<=100 under load. Correct: the check measures the pause as Flow defines it (n1 - n0 at most the one take in flight, or the pause awaited as an event), never by retry. Proofs: `bin/check backpressure` green under load."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-check-backpressure-under-load-green-pause-measured}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-swarm-task-is-closed-on-a-regression-it-did-not-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: seen once and not reproduced, its cause only inferred; a reproduction (or a second sighting with the run's store kept) has to show a defect first. Seen once, 2026-11-06, in bin/check swarm 10 10: 9 of 10 tasks merged; sw/s9 was held, its agent closed, with \"the same verdict again: regression app.proofs/s1-test\", so sw/s9-merge stayed blocked. Inferred, not shown: another task's merge moved s1-test under s9's judge, which would be the order of merges deciding a verdict. Not reproduced, and the run's store was not kept. To find: whether s9 could regress s1-test at all, or read a verdict at a digest it did not change. What should be true: a task is only closed on a regression its own branch caused. A red here is a defect in the judge or the step, not a reason to re-run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-check-step-sits-idle-until-its-limit"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: no cause shown; it needs the at-checkpoint/ sample and fiber dump bin/evidence leaves at 10 minutes from the next sighting, read first by the Check session (it may then merge into jolt.async/a-parked-fiber-nothing-can-wake-is-told or seon.repl/an-eval-that-never-returns-gives-the-model-its-turn-back). Seen twice: Context's bin/prove --merge hit its 1,200 s limit with all tests green and a rerun green in 3 minutes (2026-11-06), and on 2026-11-07 a prove of .worktrees/value-draws at 18 minutes: all shards done, bin/check's robust step idle from its start, robust.log empty. Its root at 0% CPU, main thread in a condition wait, 20 Flow procs idle on alts, 2 fibers in jolt.async.remote/rd-fill! on their sockets; its fork server and agents at 0%, one agent at 1.17 GB, which fits seon.check/robust's (count (vec (range 300000000))), the root waiting on that agent's answer. Not shown: what that agent was doing; the limit ended it seconds before it was sampled. bin/check now leaves at-checkpoint/ at 10 minutes (bin/evidence), so the next one holds its sample and fiber dump. Read those first. Correct: every wait ends, with its value or a typed error (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner's ruling, 2026-11-10 (docs/decisions.md): one test system inside Seon. Test plan step 6, after process tests run from inside. Today bin/check runs its steps as a runner of its own, each on a cp copy of a seeded check store (a copy that silently failed made fresh stores and three reds, batch 3). Plan: each mode that drives a root becomes a deftest on the service, its root on a branch of the seeded store (one store, many branches); only durable, window and resume, whose subject is what a store holds after a kill and reopen, keep a store file of their own; lmdb-kill and lmdb-sem keep their envs. bin/check's captures read stdout alone meanwhile (KNOWN=$(run steps), JOBS=$(run jobs) merge stderr). Removes in the same landing: bin/check's runner (about 320 lines) and its per-step store copies; store-steps!. Proof to write: a step's main is a branch of the seeded store, its standing targets present."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-problems-step-holds-its-stall-until-it-sees-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by the managing session in batch 9's prove (main 19443302cc + readnowrite + day2pins), at machine load 26-32: the problems check step ran 77 minutes; its root (./seon problems --profile scripted, pid 93197) at 100% CPU and 13.7 GB resident, every thread parked but one in the collector (sweep_generation); one fiber running 22.2 G ticks under seon.check/run-check; three fiber dumps in its log. The same step on the same tree alone at load 5: PROBLEMS OK five of five, 10-15 s. Evidence: /tmp/claude-501/batch9-hang/ (problems.log, sample-93197.txt, batch9-prove.log, rerun*.log). Cause (read, the Tests session): seon.check/problems-check provokes a stall by an agent eval of (Thread/sleep 2500) under :stall-ms 1000 and then waits, unnamed, for a db value holding it: (seon.root/await-db sys #(seq (stalled-rows %))). The stall is :seon.process/stall, a fact present only while it is open (seon.problems/stalled-agents), asserted when root's stall timer (seon.root's :stall-check, a clock) finds the agent quiet past the bound, retracted by its next frame. Under load the timer's check can come after the sleep answered (no stall is ever recorded), or the assert and the retract can land in one writer batch (no db value holds it); either way no commit makes the predicate true, and an unnamed await-db ends only on such a commit: a wait with no end, re-running seon.problems/health on every commit of a root that keeps writing. Not the Datahike pin's: the wait on a transient fact is the step's, whatever the pin (the pin may change how often a batch holds both). Correct: the step holds the stall open until it has seen it, the agent's eval blocking on a fifo the step writes once the stall is on main (or on root's events, tapped before the submit), so the stall is certain at any load and the wait ends; the after-wait reads the retraction, which the release makes. Sweep: the other unnamed await-db waits in seon.check wait on work that completes (a vector proc's due set emptying). What made 13.7 GB is not read yet: the sample and dumps say a root re-running health per commit, which the fix removes; if it recurs elsewhere it is its own task. Proof, red first: the step with a stall bound longer than the sleep (no stall can come) ends, with a typed error naming the wait, never hangs; and green as today at load 5."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-inputs-sees-every-clock-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 (jolt/tests-cut): `bin/check test-inputs` (test/seon/check.clj test-inputs, docs/clocks.md) does not see a read of System/currentTimeMillis, one of its own hidden-inputs: a probe test file holding (defn probe-a [] (System/currentTimeMillis)) left :unnamed [] and TEST-INPUTS OK, and two real reads went unnamed (seon.results-test's age!, seon.e2e-test/await-quiet-ends-on-a-recorded-defect; both replaced on that branch). System/getenv reads in seon.config-test are seen, so the hole is in how that call's :form/refs are recorded or queried, not read yet. A clock read in a string sent to an agent or root (Thread/sleep in seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent and seon.repl-test/a-reply-comes-after-its-commit) is no form ref at all. Correct: the check reads what the compiler records of every hidden input, so a probe of each one in hidden-inputs is red. Proof, red first: a check of test-inputs over a probe file reading each hidden input names each one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-a-quiet-root-that-cannot-come-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner of the build: Optimization. Shape agreed with Web: end await-records/await-db when root is quiet (the reconciler idle, nothing in flight) with the predicate still false, answering :seon.await/never-came with what it waited for and the last value seen; no clock. A wait on a predicate that can never become true ends only by an outside bound, against every wait ends (AGENTS.md section 4). Seen 2026-11-10 on the step-2 batch (batch 24): the mcp check's await-records (test/seon/check.clj) waited on an eval record with :seon.eval/by \"outside\", which step 2 made a ref, so it could never match, and the step hung until bin/check's new step bound ended it at 51.6 s (twice; evidence .worktrees/batch-24/target/check/20261110T231117-67541/mcp-overran). Correct (Optimization): the fact that ends such a wait honestly is one root already has, quiescence (the reconciler quiet, nothing in flight): a wait that sees root quiet with its predicate still false will never be satisfied by anything root is doing, so await-records and seon.root/await-db (and the check helpers built on them) end then with a typed error naming what they waited for and the last value they saw (:seon.await/never-came), never a clock. Owner: Optimization."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-wait-on-a-quiet-root-that-cannot-come-ends}}
]
